  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 141 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nSaturday: The warm sector gradient orients northwest to southeast \nacross Iowa, and extending further into Nebraska. The next shortwave \nwill spawn an MCS on this gradient early Saturday with its remains \nentering Iowa by Saturday afternoon. It will be caught by the \nretreating surface front, the highest confidence in afternoon severe \nstorms in southwest Iowa. The LLJ will intensify Saturday afternoon \nand overnight, igniting the elevated reflection of the front. It \nwill veer east through the night and offer a chance for heavy rain \nin central and eastern Iowa, reflected by the Marginal ERO. PWATs \nwill rise over 1.5\" and the mean wind will point to the east, \nfavoring some training on the flattening boundary. If storms lean \nmore organized, training will happen less.\n\nSunday: While dependent on weather the previous two days, Sunday \nnight looks representative of a Maddox frontal setup for heavy rain. \nWith model simulations placing the front in southern Minnesota. At \nthis range, due to cold pools from storms days prior, this QPF axis \nis possibly predicted to be too far north. PWATs will approach \n2 inches and both the mean wind and Corfidi vectors mirror the \nfront, meaning training is possible even with organized MCSs. \nIt's worth monitoring trends to see if the front does indeed \nsink south into Iowa. Rain rate-driven flash flooding would \nbecome possible in urban areas.\n\nThe forecast beyond features northwest flow with relatively higher \ndeep-layer shear in northern Iowa. This is due to enhanced flow \nacross the Upper Midwest. Precipitation chances will gradually sink \nsouth through the week due to repeated storms shoving the boundary \nmore into the ridge. Nearby instability will keep storms in\nplay.",
